    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Jurchens were culturally diverse: some groups lived as hunter-gatherers, some as semi-nomadic pastoralists and most as sedentary farmers.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taizu-of-jin">Wanyan Aguda</a> unified the Jurchen tribes in 1115 and founded the Jin dynasty, which conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liao-dynasty">Liao</a> in 1125 and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaifeng">Kaifeng</a> in 1127 before falling to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1234.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>, a Jianzhou chieftain, united the Jurchen tribes from 1586 and organised them into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eight-banners">Eight Banners</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a> renamed the people <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> in 1635.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Jin dynasty's name means &quot;gold&quot; and comes from the &quot;Gold River&quot;, Aisin in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a>, in the Jurchens' ancestral homeland.</p></aside>
    </section>

